1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:08,800
Hey everyone.  We've covered issues between divisions, but we want to focus on how those divisions work independently and interdependently.

2
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:09,840
Let's dive into it.

3
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:14,720
We are back here at NexTech and we're going to be launching our new product, the Nova Pro.

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:17,920
This is NexTech's most ambitious product yet.

5
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:19,040
Everything was already set.

6
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:24,080
Engineering had finalized the specs, Marketing was executing a multi million dollar campaign.

7
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:30,400
Sales had pre ordered agreements with key distributors, and supply chains have locked in sourcing contracts.

8
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:33,600
And still the launch was delayed by six weeks.

9
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:34,880
So what went wrong?

10
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:40,880
Each function, each division did its job, but no one owned the outcome.

11
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:45,600
In effect, NexTech created a structural failure rooted in functional silos.

12
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:54,560
Organizational units such as marketing, Engineering or operations that focus on optimizing local performance, often with limited coordination across the departments.

13
00:00:55,040 --> 00:01:02,680
Functional silos are efficient at developing depth of expertise, standardizing routines and optimizing local performance.

14
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:08,280
They enable scale by breaking work in the manageable replica units.

15
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:13,320
This is a model drawn from industrial era principles of division of labor.

16
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:16,920
However, this efficiency comes at a cost.

17
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:26,120
As silos grow in autonomy, they also fragment accountability, meaning that no single actor is responsible for end to end outcomes.

18
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:34,600
And each unit becomes focused on hitting its own performance metrics, even if those metrics are misaligned with the firm's broader strategic plan.

19
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:36,200
This results in a paradox.

20
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:40,680
Each function succeeds independently, but strategy fails collectively.

21
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:50,360
What looks like local optimization at the departmental level often translates to global sub optimization for the organization as a whole.

22
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:53,640
Silos aren't bad by design.

23
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:55,760
Division of labor is a pretty good thing.

24
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:59,680
Each unit gains depth, expertise and autonomy.

25
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:03,280
People get clear goals and leaders get measurable outcomes.

26
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:06,720
But silos have another side.

27
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:10,160
They obscure independence, interdependencies.

28
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:13,440
They shift focus from customers to internal metrics.

29
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:20,720
And they struggle when work becomes or what requires integration, like launching a complex time sensitive project.

30
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:26,280
So as coordination demands rise, silo based structures create coordination cost.

31
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:33,640
This is the time, effort and negotiation required to align decisions across autonomous units.

32
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:40,840
In our example here, Next tech is experiencing increased coordination costs and this is causing structural misalignment.

33
00:02:41,080 --> 00:02:44,600
That is where the company isn't aligning goals across itself.

34
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:47,160
NexTech needs to fix this problem.

35
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:51,160
And one way to do so is create ownership of connection points.  Points.

36
00:02:52,440 --> 00:03:02,120
According to organizational theorist Jay Galbraith, when interdependence increases, firms must evolve their structure to support lateral coordination.

37
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:10,360
Cross functional mechanisms like project teams, liaison roles and shared metrics that bridge silos and reduce execution risk.

38
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:15,880
At NexTech, Nova Pro's delays weren't due to poor execution.

39
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:19,040
They were due to structural misalignment each term.

40
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:22,640
Each team did its part, but no one was responsible for the whole.

41
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:25,600
That's where lateral coordination becomes a necessity.

42
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:29,400
NexTech can create project leaders to sync timeliness or vendor approvals.

43
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:40,320
Product teams create shared ownership, where success is defined as launch success and not departmental success or liaison roles to flag misalignments early and prevent launch delays.

44
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:46,280
This will help formalize collaboration, assign accountability and and create measurable outcomes.

45
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:49,400
Let's take a look at what happened.

46
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:53,320
Engineering signed off on the prototype, didn't check with compliance.

47
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:57,800
Procurement sourced a new chip, but the vendor had failed to pass an audit.

48
00:03:58,520 --> 00:04:03,320
Marketing booked ad buys before customer support even knew what was launching.

49
00:04:03,640 --> 00:04:07,000
Each team claimed success, but the product failed to ship on time.

50
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:11,480
This breakdown illustrates the absence of horizontal accountability.

51
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:15,520
This is a critical gap in many traditional organizations.

52
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:18,160
Most firms are built on vertical structures.

53
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:22,160
Each department is measured by its own performance.

54
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:27,760
Engineering by technical delivery, Marketing by campaign reach and operations by efficiency.

55
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:30,400
And these structures are excellent for specialization.

56
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:33,840
But they also assume values created within functions.

57
00:04:34,080 --> 00:04:39,040
When no one is explicitly responsible for coordinating across silos, I.e.

58
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:45,680
aligning goals, timing, resources and decisions that even the best strategies collapse in the gaps between teams.

59
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:47,760
This is what we call an execution gap.

60
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:52,160
That is the space between strategic intent and operational reality.

61
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:57,840
Vertical hierarchies define who does what by function or department.

62
00:04:58,400 --> 00:05:06,160
But in modern organizations, outcomes such as launching a product or delivering a customer experience cuts across those functions.

63
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:14,080
Without horizontal accountability, no one is responsible for integrating the work and execution fails at the seams.

64
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:20,160
Value is increasingly created across functions such as product development, fulfillment and service.

65
00:05:20,480 --> 00:05:28,000
But when accountability is confined to silos, teams will optimize for their own metrics and not the end to end result.

66
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:35,480
Horizontal accountability ensures someone is responsible for the full flow of value, not just the isolated contributions.

67
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:41,000
To fix this, NexTech moved to the M form structure.

68
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,320
This is a multi divisional form of organization.

69
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:48,600
Each major product, like Nova Pro becomes a semi autonomous business unit.

70
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:52,760
These units had a general manager with full profit and loss responsibility.

71
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:59,320
Embedded leads from marketing, engineering, ops and finance, and shared accountability for product performance.

72
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:06,200
Moving away from the departmental metrics now, our teams aren't organized around their own functions.

73
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:10,360
Instead, they're organized around outcomes for the organization.

74
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:25,160
An informed firm decentralizes operational decisions to divisions, each aligned with a product, geography or customer, while keeping strategy and capital allocation centralized at the corporate level.

75
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:34,640
In an inform organization, the company is divided into semi autonomous units, each responsible for a distinct product line, geography or customer segment.

76
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:44,240
These divisions have their own leadership teams and are given decision rights over Day to day operations, including production, marketing, local budgeting.

77
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:53,040
Critically, they also take profit and loss responsibility, meaning each unit is accountable for its own financial outcomes.

78
00:06:53,360 --> 00:07:05,600
At the same time, corporate headquarters retain centralized control over strategic functions such as such as capital allocation, portfolio management, long range planning and brand and policy oversight.

79
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:12,160
This dual structure works because it mirrors how value is created in diversified firms.

80
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:14,560
Let's look at each focus.

81
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:21,600
Each division can focus on a specific market or product, leading to deeper expertise and faster decision making.

82
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:31,280
Accountability Profit and loss ownership drives accountability at the divisional level, so results are no longer diluted or obscured by conglomerate reporting.

83
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:41,440
Strategic oversight headquarters can act like an internal investor evaluating divisional performance and allocating capital to where it creates the most value and scalability.

84
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:43,840
The structure can accommodate growth.

85
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:49,600
New divisions can be added as the business diversifies without overwhelming central leadership.

86
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:55,960
Now we're going to move from our service level agreements to shared success for next Tech.

87
00:07:55,960 --> 00:07:58,400
Redefining the structure was just the beginning.

88
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:01,200
It also redefined how teams were measured.

89
00:08:01,440 --> 00:08:17,040
They replaced siloed metrics like marketing impressions and procurement cross procurement cost savings with shared key performance indicators such as time to market, gross margin by product, net promoter score and post launch product reliability.

90
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:20,720
Now the Nova Pro team won or they lost together.

91
00:08:21,240 --> 00:08:25,720
But keep in mind, structure alone is not enough.

92
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:30,040
Just because teams report to the same GM doesn't mean they're truly collaborating.

93
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:35,880
As long as marketing is measured on impressions and engineering on specs, they won't naturally align.

94
00:08:36,040 --> 00:08:42,360
Hierarchy can enforce structure, but only shared goals and mutual accountability enforce collaboration.

95
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:54,440
Shared key performance indicators equal shared ownership metrics like time to market and post launch defect rate force teams to coordinate, negotiate trade offs and care about the end to end experience.

96
00:08:55,240 --> 00:09:05,480
When teams are jointly measured on outcomes like time to market product reliability, success depends on how well they work together, not just how well they perform individually.

97
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:10,360
This shifts the focus from functional excellence to cross functional execution.

98
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:16,040
And it reflects the theory accountability must match value creation.

99
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:25,000
Organizational theorists like Galbraith and Chandler showed that performance systems only work when they mirror how value is actually produced.

100
00:09:25,400 --> 00:09:31,600
If teams collaborate to deliver results, the metrics and accountability must span those same boundaries.

101
00:09:31,600 --> 00:09:35,960
If outcome span functions, the KPIs must too.

102
00:09:36,760 --> 00:09:39,960
So here are a few questions for you to consider when thinking about your own organization.

103
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:43,280
What metrics in your organization reinforce silos?

104
00:09:43,600 --> 00:09:49,920
How would your team behavior change if you shared key performance indicators across functions?

105
00:09:50,160 --> 00:09:55,120
And where are you using service level agreements when you should be using outcome metrics?

106
00:09:55,200 --> 00:10:05,200
For example, it responds to help desk tickets within 24 hours versus employee satisfaction with IT support improved by 30%.

107
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:06,840
How do you change this?

108
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:08,160
And how do you measure it?

109
00:10:09,160 --> 00:10:10,760
Let's finish up some key takeaways.

110
00:10:11,960 --> 00:10:15,240
Functional silos optimize depth, but ignore flow.

111
00:10:15,320 --> 00:10:21,800
Silos build expertise and efficiency with functions, but they often overlook how work moves across boundaries.

112
00:10:21,800 --> 00:10:27,560
The result is local excellence, but systemic friction, especially during cross functional execution.

113
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:30,520
Cross functional work requires shared ownership.

114
00:10:30,520 --> 00:10:35,480
When teams contribute to a common goal, isolated accountability breaks down.

115
00:10:35,920 --> 00:10:42,160
Shared KPIs and joint decision rights create the conditions for true collaboration and end to end performance.

116
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:46,800
Inform structures align teams with products, not just processes.

117
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:55,200
By organizing around products or business lines, informed firms give teams full visibility and control over the value they create.

118
00:10:55,280 --> 00:11:00,720
This structural focus enables autonomy while preserving strategic alignment.

119
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:06,640
Interdependence rises, so must integration, as work becomes more interconnected.

120
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:09,200
Coordination cannot rely on hierarchy alone.

121
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:13,280
Firms must add lateral mechanisms like shared metrics.

122
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:17,520
Project teams cross functional governance to keep pace with complexity.

123
00:11:17,760 --> 00:11:21,680
And lastly, strategy doesn't fail at conception, it fails at coordination.

124
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:28,680
Great strategies often fall apart in execution because no one is responsible for connecting the pieces.

125
00:11:29,080 --> 00:11:35,400
Coordination gaps, not bad plans are the silent killers of enterprise performance.  And that's it.

126
00:11:35,480 --> 00:11:36,600
Hope you've enjoyed it.